# OpenAI API Platform Provider

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

## Webhook beta status

Webhook endpoint resources and data sources are beta. Their API and Terraform
schema may change before general availability. Organization-level webhook
management is not part of this API surface.

## Authentication

The provider accepts a separate credential for each API audience:

| Terraform operations | Provider attribute | Environment variable | Required authority |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Organization administration, including every `openai_project*` resource and data source | `admin_api_key` | `OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY` | [OpenAI Admin API key](https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/admin-keys) |
| Beta project webhook resources and data sources | `api_key` | `OPENAI_API_KEY` | Project API key with `api.webhooks.read`; full lifecycle management also requires `api.webhooks.write` and project-owner authority |
| Beta tenant workload identity resources and data sources | `tenant_admin_api_key` | `OPENAI_TENANT_ADMIN_KEY` | Tenant Admin API key with `tenant.workload_identity.read`; resources also require `tenant.workload_identity.write` |
| Beta workspace workload identity resources and data sources | `workspace_admin_api_key` | `OPENAI_WORKSPACE_ADMIN_KEY` | Workspace admin personal access token with `chatgpt.workspace.workload_identity.write` |

Set the credentials for the resources in your configuration:

```sh
export OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY="<your-admin-api-key>"
export OPENAI_API_KEY="<your-project-api-key>"
export OPENAI_TENANT_ADMIN_KEY="<your-tenant-admin-api-key>"
export OPENAI_WORKSPACE_ADMIN_KEY="<your-workspace-admin-personal-access-token>"
```

Inject credentials through the runtime environment for both planning and applying,
including separate workers that apply saved plans. Do not use Terraform input
variables, `TF_VAR_admin_api_key`, `.tfvars` files, or literal provider arguments
for reusable Admin keys. Terraform's `sensitive` flag hides ordinary display;
it does not keep ordinary input values out of saved plans.

Explicit provider credential attributes remain supported for compatibility.
If explicit configuration is necessary, Terraform 1.10 or later supports input
variables marked with both `sensitive = true` and `ephemeral = true`. Supply
those values again for each operation and keep their sources out of committed
configuration and variable files. See [Terraform's sensitive data guidance](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/manage-sensitive-data).
The environment-based example below does not require Terraform 1.10.

Protect the runtime's secret storage and logs separately. Environment delivery
avoids Terraform input serialization; it does not control how a CI or hosted-run
platform stores injected secrets, and it does not make resource state non-sensitive.

Credentials are routed only to their matching audience. The provider does not
inspect a key to infer its audience and never retries an operation with
another credential. If a required credential is absent, the affected resource or
data source reports which provider attribute or environment variable to set. If
a credential is placed in the wrong attribute, it is sent only to that
attribute's API audience and the OpenAI API rejects the request.

The `project` provider attribute, or `OPENAI_PROJECT_ID`, sends the
`OpenAI-Project` header. It selects a project only for credentials that already
have access to that project; it does not grant access or convert a project key
into an organization credential.

### Custom API origins

`OPENAI_API_KEY` is used only with the default OpenAI API origin. When setting a
custom `base_url`, set `api_key` explicitly to authorize sending the project
credential to that origin.

For backward compatibility, `OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY` remains eligible for the
configured `base_url`. Only allow trusted Terraform configuration to choose a
custom origin when an Admin key is injected by the runtime. Explicit credential
attributes likewise authorize sending that credential to the configured
origin. Custom remote origins must use HTTPS, and redirects remain restricted
to the configured origin.

`OPENAI_TENANT_ADMIN_KEY` is used only with the default OpenAI API origin.
Use `tenant_base_url` to override the tenant API endpoint. For a custom
origin, set `tenant_admin_api_key` explicitly to authorize sending the
tenant credential there. Organization and project settings do not select
the tenant for these requests.

Workspace operations use `https://api.chatgpt.com/v1`. Set `workspace_base_url`
to override it. `OPENAI_WORKSPACE_ADMIN_KEY` is limited to the default origin;
for a custom origin, explicitly set `workspace_admin_api_key`. Workspace
requests do not send Platform organization or project headers.

### Webhook lifecycle limitations

The webhook signing secret is returned only during creation and is stored as a
sensitive value in Terraform state. Protect the state backend. Import cannot
recover the secret, and rotating it outside Terraform leaves the stored value
stale. Endpoint test delivery and signing-secret rotation are imperative API
operations; use the API or an SDK for those actions instead of Terraform.

## Tenant workload identity (beta)

Use `openai_tenant_workload_identity_provider` to manage tenant trust settings and
`openai_tenant_workload_identity_federation_rule` to map an external identity to an
existing principal. Each rule requires exactly one `workspace_target` or
`api_organization_target`. The target workspace or organization must already be
linked to the tenant.

The matching singular data sources read an existing provider or rule. The plural
`openai_tenant_workload_identity_providers` and
`openai_tenant_workload_identity_federation_rules` data sources read all pages and
return typed `data` summaries. Individual and list reads use the same named
target objects; the inactive target is null. Arbitrary JSON fields (JWKS, claims,
and attribute matchers) are available through individual data sources or the
lists' complete `response_json`.

Import a provider with its provider ID. Import a rule with `provider_id/rule_id`.
Changing a rule's target identity replaces the rule. Changing matchers or
permissions updates the existing rule.

## Workspace workload identity (beta)

Use `openai_workspace_workload_identity_provider` and
`openai_workspace_workload_identity_federation_rule` for Codex configuration
owned by a ChatGPT workspace. Each resource takes `workspace_id`. A rule also
takes `workload_identity_provider_id` and `principal_id`, which must identify an
existing user or service account in that workspace. Supply an `external_subject`,
`claims`, or a CEL `condition` to match the external identity.

Verification defaults to discovery. A `verification` object can instead select
`type = "uploaded_jwks"` with `jwks`, or `type = "jwks_uri"` with `jwks_uri`.
Set `verification = { type = "discovery" }` to clear configured keys or a URI.

Import a provider with `workspace_id/provider_id` and a rule with
`workspace_id/provider_id/rule_id`. Changing a rule's workspace, provider, or
principal replaces it. Changing matchers or permissions updates it in place.
Internal binding IDs are resolved automatically.

The matching singular and plural data sources read configuration visible to
the workspace, including shared tenant configuration. The plural data sources
return the complete list in `data`; arbitrary JSON is available through
individual data sources and the lists' `response_json`. Use tenant resources
to change shared tenant configuration.

## Project roles

Projects include the following built-in roles:

- Project member: `role-api-project-member`.
- Project owner: `role-api-project-owner`.
- Project viewer: `role-api-project-viewer`.

Assign a built-in role to a user with `openai_project_user_role`:

```terraform
resource "openai_project_user_role" "member" {
  project_id = var.project_id
  user_id    = var.user_id
  role_id    = "role-api-project-member"
}
```

Assign a built-in role to a group with `openai_project_group_role`:

```terraform
resource "openai_project_group_role" "member" {
  project_id = var.project_id
  group_id   = var.group_id
  role_id    = "role-api-project-member"
}
```

Use `openai_project_roles` to discover the roles available to a project
dynamically:

```terraform
data "openai_project_roles" "available" {
  project_id = var.project_id
}

locals {
  member_role_id = one([
    for role in data.openai_project_roles.available.roles :
    role.id
    if role.predefined_role && role.name == "member"
  ])
}
```

Use `local.member_role_id` as the `role_id` in either assignment resource to
assign the dynamically discovered built-in member role.

